insert into public.academy_course_structures
    (app_slug, tier, structure, content_version)
insert into public.academy_quiz_questions
    (app_slug, tier, scope, module_key, ord, prompt, options, answer_index, explanation)
values
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 1, 'The published taper settles a buoyed weight of 8673.76 lb, a spring rate of 267.09 lb/in and a fundamental of 53.3621 spm before a single time step is taken. Which quantity cannot be had the same way?', '["The weight in air of 9940 lb, since a weight per foot times a length is a catalog lookup rather than a closed form.", "The plunger area of 2.40528 in2, because an area belongs to the pump rather than to the string that carries it.", "The minimum polished rod load, which is read off a card the march has to produce first.", "The largest torque factor of 56.3053 in, since differentiating a closure means turning the crank through a whole revolution first."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Weight, stiffness, the note, the stroke, the area and the fluid load are closed form and timeless. Both polished rod loads, the plunger stroke, the card area and the horsepower wait on a march.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 2, 'Rated displacement on the teaching well ODUMA-4 reads 380.874 bbl/d. Which of the four objects owns that number?', '["The pump, since it is a diameter squared times a stroke times a speed times 0.116571 and nothing is marched to get it.", "The card, because a displacement is what a well actually delivers, and only a solved card knows how far the plunger really travelled in a stroke.", "The linkage, since the 106.688 in of surface stroke standing inside that product came out of the four-bar closure and out of nowhere else at all.", "The string, because the 17.5607 in of stretch the fluid load causes is already inside the figure."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Fluid load, plunger area, displacement, the groups and the Goodman check all belong to rodPumpDesign, which is closed form until it calls the card solver.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 3, 'A 7/8 rod carries a stress wave at 16288.8 ft/s while Young''s modulus and steel density alone give 16981.9 ft/s. What accounts for the gap?', '["The fluid the string hangs in, which adds an apparent mass and slows every wave travelling down a submerged rod.", "The damping ratio the march is given, which the catalog fixes at 1.087 and applies to the acoustic velocity.", "The taper factor of 1.09201, which raises a stepped string''s note and lowers the velocity that produced it.", "Published rod weights include couplings, so the mass per foot is higher than bare steel while the area carrying the stiffness is not."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The catalog states a coupling allowance of 1.087, and the bare steel velocity divided by its square root gives 16288.1 ft/s.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 4, 'Put a 3/4 section above a 7/8 section over the same 5000 ft. What comes back?', '["A refusal with one error, since a section larger than the one above it cannot carry what hangs below it.", "An answer with ok true, a taperStepsUp warning and a spring rate of 251.237 lb/in.", "The same 267.09 lb/in as the sensible order, because a compliance sum is indifferent to the order the sections arrive in.", "An answer with the sections silently sorted into descending size and no warning attached."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A reversed taper is a design error rather than a parse error, so the engine computes it and says so with a code.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 5, 'Ask for a rod size of seven eighths, spelled out in words. What happens, and why does it matter?', '["It is refused with one error quoting the string back, because a bare numeric parse of 7/8 with the slash swapped for a dot gives 7.8 in and an area of 47.7836 in2 against the true 0.60132 in2.", "It defaults to the nearest catalog size and warns, so the design continues on 7/8 rods with a note attached.", "It is refused, and the point is tidy input handling rather than anything a wrong area would have done downstream.", "It is accepted and returns 0.60132 in2, since the catalog holds spelled out names beside the fractions."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The area factor is 79.4645, and a string with eighty times its true area barely stretches, so every load and stroke after it would have been confidently wrong.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 6, 'The engine reproduces the oracle with a difference of 0.000e+0 on weight in air, buoyed weight, elastic constant and spring rate. What does that establish?', '["That the card march is validated to the same exactness, because one test file carries both sets of values and both were signed off together.", "That the published taper''s figures were measured on a well and reproduced by calculation.", "That two routes agree about arithmetic, since the oracle''s gates hold them only to 2 percent on plunger stroke and 3 percent on the minimum load.", "That any disagreement below 2 percent anywhere in the package would have been caught by now, a compliance sum on the same string having come back exact."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A compliance sum and Archimedes each have exactly one answer, so exactness there is agreement about arithmetic and not evidence about a well.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 7, 'The engine builds IN3_PER_BBL as 9702 in3 per bbl from 42 gallons of 231 in3 rather than storing the displacement constant outright. What does that buy?', '["Accuracy, since a stored constant would be rounded and a built one carries every figure the definition has.", "Unit flexibility, because the same chain of 42 gallons of 231 in3 can be rebuilt in cubic metres whenever a design has to be reported in metric.", "Nothing at all, since 9702 in3 per bbl is a definition and two routes to a definition must agree by construction.", "The oracle builds the same 0.116571 starting from gallons, so a slip in either chain shows as a disagreement instead of a shared error."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two independent chains to 0.116571 bbl per day per in2 per in per spm is the whole point of an oracle that shares no code with the engine.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 8, 'A design comes back with no warnings at all. What has it actually been told?', '["That every failure mode the engine models is clear, including the compression a sinker bar would be sized for.", "That it passed the questions this engine asks, and nothing about buckling, gas interference or rod on tubing wear.", "That its fatigue life clears the modified Goodman line with margin, rodOverstressed being the code that would have said otherwise.", "That the string, the linkage and the pump are each free of every error the package is capable of detecting."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Rod buckling, tubing movement, fluid friction, gas interference, side loading and gearbox losses are not modelled, so no number in the output speaks to any of them.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 9, 'A damping ratio of zero comes back with ok false. What kind of refusal is that, and what does the message add?', '["A physical one, since with no damping the string never settles into a repeating stroke, and the text names the field range of about 0.05 to 0.15 of critical.", "A parse refusal, because zero falls outside the numeric range the input validator accepts for a ratio.", "A physical one, and the message offers no remedy because there is nothing a caller could reasonably put in its place.", "A warning rather than a refusal, since an undamped march still returns a card and flags it as notPeriodic."]'::jsonb, 0, 'It is one of the eight refusals, and each of the eight carries the engine''s own text with a remedy in it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 10, 'Why are the three tiers of this course cut where they are?', '["The first three objects were validated against the oracle and the fourth was not, so the card waits until the tolerances are understood.", "Closed form work is arithmetic and a march is physics, so the seam falls between what a designer computes and what a simulator does.", "The first three objects are cheap to compute and the card is expensive, so the tiers are split on how long an answer takes to produce.", "Nothing the string, the linkage or the pump owns needs a march, and everything the card owns does."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A compliance sum, a buoyancy factor, an eigenvalue, a four-bar closure and a differential times an area are the same at 5 spm and at 15 spm.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 11, 'runRodPumpDesign refuses ODUMA-4 at 60 spm. Why can only the joining function make that particular refusal?', '["Because the pump is where every rate in the design is computed, and a pumping speed becomes a number worth checking only once a displacement is asked for.", "Because the card solver is the only routine that would notice a march failing to settle at that speed.", "The speed is being checked against the string''s own fundamental of 59.1343 spm, and no single object holds both numbers.", "Because a four-bar cannot be closed at a crank turning 60 times a minute, so the geometry has to be checked before that speed can be accepted anywhere."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It takes the string, the unit, the pump and the fluid together, so it is the only place a reason belonging to two objects at once can be raised.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 12, 'predictCard and diagnoseCard sit in the same file and share no code path. What is that worth?', '["One can be checked against the other, since a march down the string and harmonics propagated up from a measured card are independent routes.", "Speed, because a diagnostic that reused the march would have to run the march before it could read anything.", "Nothing beyond convenience, since the caller simply picks whichever matches the data on hand.", "It keeps the approximation well away from the measurement, the measured card being the authority on the well and the marched card being only an estimate of it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'predictCard marches the damped wave equation and diagnoseCard propagates Fourier harmonics of a measured card, which is why either can test the other.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 13, 'What is wrong with putting ODUMA-4''s 380.874 bbl/d on a design sheet as the well''s rate?', '["It is quoted at 10 spm, and a rating means nothing without its speed.", "It is computed on the 106.688 in surface stroke, which the plunger never travels.", "It belongs to a teaching well rather than a published case, so it carries no oracle behind it.", "It uses a plunger area of 2.40528 in2 where the displacement constant wants a diameter squared, so it is low by 21.4602 percent."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A rating assumes a full barrel and the whole surface stroke, and how much shorter the plunger''s own travel is belongs to the fourth object.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 14, 'Which condition returns an answer carrying a warning code rather than ok false?', '["A pumping speed of zero, since a cycle of no length still leaves a card the engine can decimate.", "A measured card with fewer than sixteen samples, which is propagated with a note that the harmonics are thin.", "A plunger whose discharge does not exceed its intake, which returns a fluid load of 0 lb beside the warning.", "A fillage below 0.85, which raises incompleteFillage and still reports a production."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The eight refusals return no numbers at all, while taperStepsUp, timestep, notPeriodic, rodOverstressed, structuralOverload, torqueOverload, strokeOverload and incompleteFillage all return an answer and say so.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm01-a-well-on-a-beam', 15, 'The published taper stretches 18.7202 in under a fluid load of 5000 lb. Why is that not yet an answer for how far the plunger travels?', '["Because the stretch has to be counted twice, once for the upstroke and once for the downstroke.", "Because the buoyancy factor of 0.872611 has not yet been applied to the stretch.", "The string is a wave guide as well as a spring, so the motion arrives at the pump late, reflects and returns while the polished rod is still travelling.", "Because 5000 lb is a fluid load rather than the buoyed weight of 8673.76 lb the polished rod carries at rest."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A 7/8 rod carries a stress wave at 16288.8 ft/s, so what the plunger does is the answer to a wave problem and takes a march to get.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 1, 'The published taper''s two sections have compliances of 1.962893216e-3 and 1.781143844e-3 in/lb. What is the string spring rate, and what had to be done to get it?', '["267.091373300 lb/in, by adding the two compliances and inverting the sum.", "1070.889032254 lb/in, by adding the two section spring rates, which is what an arrangement of springs side by side would call for.", "509.452063888 lb/in, the rate of the 3000 ft of 7/8 standing alone, because the longest section is the one that governs a string built in series.", "3.744037060e-3 lb/in, the sum of the compliances read straight off, a compliance in in/lb and a spring rate in lb/in being one number written either way."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Compliances add in series and stiffnesses do not: 3.744037060e-3 in/lb inverts to 267.091373300 lb/in, and adding rates gives 1070.889032254 lb/in, 4.009448 times too stiff.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 2, 'You compute a spring rate for a two section string and get a number larger than either section''s own rate. What has happened?', '["The sections step up in size, which is the condition taperStepsUp exists for, and the engine returns the stiffened rate with the code attached.", "The buoyancy factor was left out of the sum, and a string computed in air comes back stiffer than the same string weighed in fluid.", "Spring rates were added instead of compliances, because a series string is always softer than its softest section.", "Nothing at all, since two stiff sections working together can carry more load per inch of stretch than either of them manages on its own."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The published taper''s softest section alone would give 509.452063888 lb/in and the whole string comes out at 267.091373300 lb/in, well under it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 3, 'ODUMA-4 is 1500 ft of 1 in rods, 1600 ft of 7/8 and 1700 ft of 3/4, three nearly equal lengths, yet its compliance shares are 22.685963, 31.606022 and 45.708015 percent. Why so uneven?', '["The bottom section carries the fluid load and the top carries only rods, so the load each holds sets its share.", "Compliance per foot goes as one over the area, and the three areas are 0.785398163, 0.601320469 and 0.441786467 in2.", "Compliance per foot goes as one over the diameter, so the shares follow the three sizes in order.", "Published rod weights include couplings, and the catalog''s coupling allowance of 1.087 falls hardest on the smallest rods in the string."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A section''s compliance is its length over its area times the modulus, so the narrowest rod stretches most per foot wherever it sits: 45.708015 percent of the whole from 1700 ft of 3/4.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 4, 'Walk the split of a 5000 ft two size string from all 3/4 to all 7/8. What does a designer sweeping that table for the best row find?', '["A best row at the published 3000 ft split, where 18.720185 in of stretch under 5000 lb first stops falling as fast as the weight is rising.", "A best row at 2500 ft of 7/8, where the fundamental turns at 53.642386647 spm, so the string is lightest for the note it rings at.", "A best row at all 3/4, since 8170.000000 lb is the least steel the string can be built from and what the polished rod lifts follows the weight.", "No best row, because weight and stiffness climb together at every step, from 8170.000000 lb and 224.574787346 lb/in to 11120.000000 lb and 305.671238333 lb/in."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Every foot of 7/8 put at the top adds stiffness and adds weight in the same breath, which is why the split is settled on stress section by section rather than on either column.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 5, 'The lightest string in that sweep, all 3/4 over the full 5000 ft, weighs 8170.000000 lb. What does choosing it cost?', '["22.264298 in of stretch under 5000 lb against 16.357443 in for the heaviest split, and stretch at the top is stroke the plunger never gets.", "Nothing a design would notice, since the whole sweep moves the stretch by well under an inch and no field instrument would resolve a margin that small.", "The lowest fundamental in the sweep at 48.865672625 spm, which is the number every design on that string is refused against.", "1266.242038 lb of buoyancy, because a string with less steel in it displaces less fluid and Archimedes hands back less of the weight."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The softest string in the sweep is also the lightest: 22.264298 in of stretch at 224.574787346 lb/in against 16.357443 in at 305.671238333 lb/in, and steel saved at the top is paid for at the bottom.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 6, 'The published taper and the published uniform string hang in the same fluid of specific gravity 1.00. What buoyancy factor does each get?', '["0.872611464968 for the taper and less for the uniform string, whose 6000 ft displaces more fluid than 5000 ft.", "0.872611464968 for the uniform string and more for the taper, which has less steel per foot to displace.", "0.872611464968 for both, since the factor is one minus the fluid specific gravity over 7.85 and nothing about the string enters it.", "Neither of them gets a single factor, because buoyancy is applied section by section and only a string of one size has one factor for the whole of it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The factor is Archimedes and nothing else, so one number takes 13344.000000 lb to 11644.127388535 lb and 9940.000000 lb to 8673.757961783 lb.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 7, 'A predecessor used one minus 1.2 times the fluid specific gravity over 7.85. At specific gravity 1.00 what does that do to the published taper, and why is it hard to catch?', '["It removes about a fifth of the buoyed weight, the same 21.460184 percent the predecessor''s plunger area form takes off a rated displacement.", "It removes 253.248408 lb, 2.919708 percent of the buoyed weight, which reads as rounding, and it makes the string look lighter than it is.", "It removes 253.248408 lb, and it is easy to catch, since the error grows to 291.235669 lb at specific gravity 1.15.", "It adds 253.248408 lb, so every load computed from it starts high and the design is conservative."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The 1.2 factor gives 0.847133757962 against the true 0.872611464968, and the error runs 2.919708 percent at specific gravity 1.00 and 3.432836 percent at 1.15.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 8, 'At specific gravity 1.00 the fluid carries 1266.242038 lb of the published taper. What is that figure not?', '["The load the polished rod carries at rest, which is the buoyed weight of 8673.757961783 lb and not the part the fluid took.", "A load that changes between the upstroke and the downstroke, since Archimedes acts on a body being lifted through a fluid and not on one standing still in it.", "A number the tubing leaves alone, because an annulus removes part of the column and with it part of the buoyant force on the rods inside.", "The difference between 9940.000000 lb in air and the buoyed weight, which is what the fluid holds up."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Buoyancy is a statement about a body at rest in a fluid: it takes 9940.000000 lb down to 8673.757961783 lb, and the difference between the two is what the fluid holds up.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 9, 'A 7/8 rod is published at 2.224000 lb/ft and computes to 2.046159929 lb/ft from its area and a steel density of 490 lb/ft3. Which figure does a string weight use?', '["The computed one, since couplings are counted in the tally of rods rather than folded into a weight per foot.", "The published one, because the catalog applies its stated coupling allowance of 1.087 to every size alike, so the ratios come out identical.", "Either one, since the two agree to well inside the 2 percent the oracle holds the two routes to and no load in the design turns on the gap.", "The published one, because it includes the couplings."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Published weight over bare steel is 1.086914062177 on a 7/8 rod, and those ratios neither match the stated 1.087 nor climb with size: 5/8 is highest at 1.087207294388.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 10, 'The published taper is 6672.000000 lb of 7/8 over 3268.000000 lb of 3/4. What is its average weight per foot good for?', '["Nothing, because an average over two sections of different length has no defensible meaning and the engine does not report one.", "Rebuilding the string as a uniform 5000 ft bar at 1.988000000 lb/ft, which returns the same 267.091373300 lb/in the taper has.", "Describing the string, since no average weight can reproduce a spring rate that goes section by section as one over the area.", "Comparing it with ODUMA-4, whose 2.227541667 lb/ft comes out near it because both strings mix sizes between 3/4 and 1 in."]'::jsonb, 2, '9940.000000 lb over 5000 ft is 1.988000000 lb/ft, and it is a summary rather than an input: compliance is summed section by section from the areas 0.601320469 and 0.441786467 in2.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 11, 'The taper is 3404.000000 lb lighter in air than the published uniform string, 25.509592 percent, and stiffer by 12.365341 lb/in. What does that pair of figures not establish?', '["Anything whatever, since a weight in air and a spring rate are different quantities and two percentages taken on different units cannot be read side by side.", "What the taper itself buys, because the uniform string is 6000 ft and the taper is 5000 ft, so most of both gaps is the thousand feet.", "That the taper is stiffer, because a string carrying 2000 ft of 3/4 rods cannot be stiffer than one built of 7/8 rods over its whole length.", "That the taper is lighter, since the comparison is on weight in air and the polished rod carries 8673.757961783 lb."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The honest same length comparison is the split sweep, which holds 5000 ft fixed and moves the split from 224.574787346 lb/in to 305.671238333 lb/in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 12, 'A reversed taper comes back with a spring rate and a warning code, while a rod size the parser cannot read comes back with nothing at all. What separates the two?', '["The compliance sum sees the order and repairs it, sorting the sections into descending size before it adds them, so only the size is left to refuse.", "A warning is what the engine raises when it is unsure of an answer and a refusal is what it raises when it is certain, which is a matter of confidence.", "The reversed taper falls inside the 2 percent the oracle holds the two routes to, and a size that cannot be parsed falls outside it.", "A reversed order is a design error the arithmetic can still be carried out for, and an unreadable size leaves no area to compute anything with."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The compliance sum is indifferent to order and returns 251.236634246 lb/in against 267.091373300 lb/in for the sensible order, while a size that is not a diameter has no area at all.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 13, 'Why does refusing a rod size of seven eighths matter more than refusing most bad inputs?', '["A bare numeric parse reads 7/8 as 7.8 in, an area of 47.783624 in2 against the true 0.601320469 in2, so the string barely stretches and every load and stroke after it is quietly wrong.", "Because a rod size is the only input the string module takes, so a size it cannot read leaves the engine with nothing at all to compute with.", "Because an area 79.464490 times too large would drive the polished rod load far past the unit''s structural capacity, where the overload check would have caught it anyway.", "Because the catalog carries spelled out names beside the fractions, so a failure here is a lookup that missed rather than a parse that went wrong."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The area factor is 79.464490, and a string that stiff shortens the stretch and lengthens everything computed from it, with no warning code anywhere to flag it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 14, 'Which of these five numbers on the published taper moves when the pumping speed doubles: weight in air 9940.000000 lb, buoyed weight 8673.757961783 lb, elastic constant 3.744037060e-3 in/lb, spring rate 267.091373300 lb/in, fundamental 53.362124006 spm?', '["The fundamental of 53.362124006 spm, since a natural frequency is measured per minute and doubling the strokes a minute moves it against the same clock.", "The buoyed weight of 8673.757961783 lb, which rises with speed because the string is being accelerated through the fluid it is hanging in.", "Not one of the five, because every one of them describes steel hanging in a fluid.", "The elastic constant and the spring rate, which stiffen with speed as the steel is loaded faster than it is able to relax."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Change the speed, the damping ratio, the fillage or the plunger diameter and all five stand where they were, which is why this tier can be settled before any card is marched.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm02-the-rod-string', 15, 'A string comes back with no warnings and every closed form matching the oracle to a difference of 0.000e+0. What has it not been told?', '["Anything about the order of its sections, which a compliance sum cannot see and which no code in the package reports.", "Anything about rod buckling, tubing movement, fluid friction, gas interference, side loading or rod on tubing wear, none of which is modelled here.", "Anything about its fatigue life, which the modified Goodman percentage reports directly and which therefore needs no separate check.", "Anything about buoyancy, which is applied after the warnings have been collected and so can never contribute one of its own."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Exactness on a compliance sum is agreement about arithmetic and not evidence about a well, and the oracle''s own gates hold the two routes only to 2 percent on plunger stroke.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 1, 'The published uniform string is 7/8 rods over 6000 ft and carries a stress wave at 16288.760984482 ft/s. What does the quarter wave give, and what else went into it?', '["40.721902461206 spm, from the velocity, the length and the 13344.000000 lb the string weighs in air, since a heavier bar rings lower.", "40.721902461206 spm, from sixty times the velocity over four times the length, and nothing else enters at all.", "40.833333333 spm, because a fixed free bar rings at a quarter wavelength over its length and the field rule of 245000 over the depth is that closed form written out.", "122.165707384 spm, since the fundamental of a bar held at one end is the first mode above the rigid body motion and the quarter wave is the second."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Only the length and the wave speed enter, which is why the engine returns 40.721902461206 spm and reports uniform true with a taper factor of 1.000000000.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 2, 'A uniform 5000 ft string of 3/4 rods rings at 48.865672625 spm and a uniform 5000 ft string of 7/8 rods at 48.866282953 spm, though they weigh 8170.000000 and 11120.000000 lb. Why so close?', '["The two notes are close because both strings are 5000 ft long and the taper factor of 1.092008382351 is what would separate them.", "The extra steel in the 7/8 string is carried by the fluid it hangs in, so the two strings ring at the mass the buoyancy factor of 0.872611464968 leaves.", "A fundamental is set by the softest section, and neither string has a section softer than the other since each is built of one size.", "Stiffness and mass per foot both scale with the area, so the diameter drops out and only the wave speed is left, 16288.557542 ft/s against 16288.760984 ft/s."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The published weights of 2.224000 and 1.634000 lb/ft differ by far more than the two wave speeds do, and it is the wave speed that the note is built from.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 3, 'The field shorthand of 245000 over the depth gives 40.833333333 spm on the published uniform string. What is wrong with using it on the published taper?', '["It carries no taper factor at all, so on a string with sections it is answering a different question.", "It is 0.111430872 spm away from the engine''s answer, which is already too large a margin for a gate that refuses a design.", "It is built from an acoustic velocity of 16333 ft/s rather than the 16288.760984482 ft/s a 7/8 rod actually carries, so it drifts with rod size.", "It gives a number above the engine''s answer on the uniform string and would give one below it on the taper, so the error changes sign with the string."]'::jsonb, 0, 'On the uniform string the shorthand sits 0.111430872 spm from the engine''s 40.721902461206 spm, close enough to sketch with, and the published taper''s own factor of 1.092008382351 is nowhere in it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 4, 'Three routes give the uniform string''s fundamental: an oracle finite element eigenvalue at 40.722116061 spm, the engine scan at 40.721902461206 spm and a dense mode scan at 40.721902461 spm. What does the agreement establish?', '["That the engine scan is laying its 400 points evenly, since a grid with a defect in it could not have landed on the oracle''s answer.", "That the published uniform string was measured in a well and the three calculations reproduce what the instrument recorded there.", "That the eigenvalue is being solved consistently by independent methods, a difference of -2.1360e-4 spm being far below anything a design turns on.", "That the taper is validated to the same standard, one test file carrying both strings and the oracle gates holding both to 2 percent."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The engine scan and the dense mode scan walk the same transfer matrix, and the oracle solves a different problem entirely, so -2.1360e-4 spm is three routes to one eigenvalue.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 5, 'The published uniform string returns mode ratios of 3.000000000 and 5.000000000 and the published taper returns 2.693424993 and 4.578723505. Which of the two is the special case?', '["The taper, since a stepped bar is what the engine is built for and a single size is a degenerate string it handles by exception.", "The uniform string, because odd multiples of the fundamental are what one material and one area give in closed form.", "Neither, because both sets of ratios are fitted from the same scan and the difference between them is the resolution of the grid.", "The taper, whose ratios are pulled off three by the 1.092008382351 taper factor, which multiplies every mode alike."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Nothing is fitted in the uniform case: one material and one area give 40.721902461, 122.165707384 and 203.609512306 spm, and the numerics land on the odd harmonics exactly.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 6, 'For a stepped string the engine computes a base note first. What is the base note on the published taper, and what happens to it?', '["48.866038821915 spm, the uniform quarter wave the string would have if it were all one size, multiplied by a taper factor of 1.092008382351.", "48.866038821915 spm, the note of the softest section of the string standing on its own, which the taper factor then corrects for the sections above it.", "48.865672625 spm, the note of the all 3/4 string of the same length, since the base has to be the slowest case the taper can reduce to.", "53.362124006 spm, the note the scan bisects to, which the taper factor of 1.092008382351 is then reported beside as a diagnostic."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The base is 48.866038821915 spm and the factor is 1.092008382351, and the product is the 53.362124006 spm the engine reports for the published taper.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 7, 'Across the 5000 ft split sweep the spring rate climbs steadily from 224.574787346 to 305.671238333 lb/in. What does the fundamental do?', '["It climbs with the spring rate throughout, since a note is a stiffness over a mass and the stiffness is what is being changed.", "It stays within a few hundredths of 48.865672625 spm, because the length is held at 5000 ft and length is the only lever on a note.", "It falls steadily to 48.866282953 spm, the added steel raising the mass faster than it raises the stiffness at every step of the sweep.", "It rises to 53.642386647 spm at 2500 ft of 7/8 and then falls back, ending at 48.866282953 spm where the string is stiffest of all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The published taper at 267.091373300 lb/in rings at 53.362124006 spm, below the softer 2500 ft split, so a reader who expects the stiffest string to ring highest is wrong across the whole sweep.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 8, 'ODUMA-4 has three sections and returns a taper factor of 1.161673288802 against the published taper''s 1.092008382351. What is that comparison worth?', '["It shows ODUMA-4 to be the stiffer string of the two, since a taper factor is a ratio of stiffnesses and 1.161673288802 is the larger of the pair.", "It shows nothing, because the two strings are different lengths and a taper factor cannot be compared across lengths.", "It shows more steps giving a larger factor, and the factor is what a stepped bar''s departure from a uniform one is being measured by.", "It shows ODUMA-4 ringing lower, the larger factor dividing a base note of 50.904388515803 spm rather than multiplying it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'ODUMA-4 goes from a base note of 50.904388515803 spm to a fundamental of 59.134268422 spm, and its modes at 152.571313745 and 246.782140532 spm are no nearer the odd harmonics than the taper''s are.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 9, 'Ask ODUMA-4 for 60 spm against its own fundamental of 59.134268422 spm. What comes back?', '["An answer carrying a notPeriodic warning, since a march driven at resonance never settles into a repeating stroke but still returns a card.", "ok false with one error naming the number, and no card, no loads and no stroke of any kind.", "ok false with one error, and a plunger stroke and a rated displacement, since those two are closed form and do not need the march that was refused.", "An answer with a strokeOverload warning, because a design at the fundamental drives the polished rod past the stroke the unit is built for."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The refusal is hard and it names the speed, and the highest speed that string will accept is anything strictly below 59.134268422 spm.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 10, 'Why is a design at or above the string''s fundamental refused rather than warned about?', '["Because the alternative is a number: a march driven there would come back with the same field names and units as an answer from a sound design.", "Because a warning code has no room for a message, and this is the one condition whose remedy needs three levers named in prose.", "Because the march would not converge in the cycles it is given, so there would be no result to attach a warning to in the first place.", "Because the fundamental is a golden value, and the engine refuses any input that would put one of the golden cases outside the 2 percent the oracle holds."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The message names the speed and offers the three levers the fundamental actually has, which are slowing the unit, shortening the string and running a stiffer taper.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 11, 'ODUMA-4 runs at 10 spm against a fundamental of 59.134268422 spm, a ratio of 0.169106683. What does that ratio tell you?', '["That the design has 0.169106683 of its speed margin left, so it could be run about six times faster before the refusal fires.", "That the loads on this string will be close to static, since a ratio that far below one leaves no inertia in the answer to speak of.", "That the string is oversized for the duty, a working design being expected to sit within a few spm of its own fundamental.", "That the design sits far below its string''s note and nowhere near the second mode either, which stands 93.437045323 spm above the first."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A real design at a working speed is nowhere near the fundamental or anything above it, and the published taper''s own gap to its second mode is 90.364754474 spm.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 12, 'What does the speed gate not check?', '["Whether the string was given in a sensible order, which is left to taperStepsUp and never reaches the speed check at all.", "Whether the design is close to the limit, which it reports as a margin in spm beside the answer for every design that passes.", "Whether the number it is checking against is the string''s real fundamental, since it can only use the number the scan returned.", "Whether the speed is below the second mode as well, so a design sitting between the first and the second mode is accepted without any comment."]'::jsonb, 2, 'It is one speed against one number and a strict inequality, so a design just under its fundamental passes exactly as ODUMA-4 does at a ratio of 0.169106683, with nothing in the output to say which.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 13, 'The scan means to lay 400 evenly spaced points from 2.443302 to 195.464155 spm on the published taper, an intended spacing of 0.482552133 spm. What does the grid actually do?', '["It adds the increment to the running position rather than to the lower bound, so the spacing grows by one increment every step and 28 of the 401 points land inside the range.", "It lays the points evenly but on a logarithmic scale, so the spacing grows with the frequency and the first twelve intervals run from 0.482552 to 5.790626 spm.", "It stops as soon as it meets a sign change, so only the points below 53.362124006 spm are ever evaluated and the rest of the range is never visited.", "It doubles the increment whenever no sign change has been met, which is why the last point evaluated sits at 3.870312e+4 spm."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The first twelve intervals are 0.482552, 0.965104, 1.447656 spm and on upward, each a whole multiple of the intended one, and the last point evaluated is 1.9801e+2 times the top of the range.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 14, 'Which comparison decides whether that scan can be trusted on a string, and how does the published taper come out on it?', '["The intended spacing against the achieved spacing: 0.482552133 spm against 13.028907601 spm, a factor of 27.000000 that no bisection can recover from.", "The widest interval against the smallest gap between roots: 13.028907601 spm against 90.364754474 spm, so no interval can hold two roots.", "The engine scan against the dense mode scan: both return 53.362124006 spm, and identical answers are what says the grid is sound.", "The last point evaluated against the top of the intended range: 3.870312e+4 spm against 195.464155 spm, which is where the missing points went."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Two roots inside one interval leave the sign unchanged at both ends, so what matters is whether any interval is as wide as the 90.364754474 spm between the first and second modes.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm03-the-string-has-a-note', 15, 'Suppose a string did have two modes inside one of those intervals. What would the engine report?', '["An error, since the bisection has no bracket to work in once the sign fails to change across the interval.", "The true fundamental anyway, because the bisection that follows the scan searches the whole range rather than the one interval.", "A fundamental with the notPeriodic code attached, that being the flag the package raises whenever a solver cannot resolve what it was asked.", "A higher mode returned as the fundamental, with unresolved reading false, which is what a clean answer looks like as well."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The flag reads false on the published taper too, so a wrong fundamental would be a wrong refusal or a wrong permission and it would be quiet about it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 1, 'On the published unit the largest miss on either circle across a whole revolution is -2.842e-14 in. What follows from a residual that size?', '["The solver has converged tightly on this unit, and a geometry nearer the limit of closure would need the tolerance opened before it returned.", "The residual is the sampling error of the 360 crank steps, and finer steps would drive it lower.", "The closure is an intersection of two circles rather than a fit, so no tolerance was used and none can be loosened.", "The unit dimensions were chosen so the linkage closes exactly, and a real manufacturer''s beam would leave a residual a designer has to judge."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The equalizer bearing has to sit at 64 in about the saddle bearing and at 80 in about the crank pin at once, and intersecting those two circles is the whole solution.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 2, 'The beam on the published unit sweeps 1.000197032783 rad. How does that become a polished rod stroke?', '["Multiplied by the front arm of 106.6667 in, giving 106.687716837 in.", "Multiplied by the rear arm of 64 in and then by the ratio of the front arm to the rear arm, which is how a beam turns an angle into travel.", "Multiplied by twice the crank radius of 28.8 in, the sweep being the fraction of the crank''s own circle that reaches the polished rod.", "Multiplied by the pitman of 80 in, since the pitman is what carries the crank pin''s motion up to the beam in the first place."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The stroke is the beam angle sweep times the front arm and nothing else: 1.000197032783 rad by 106.6667 in is 106.687716837 in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 3, 'The engine gives a stroke of 106.687716837 in and the oracle''s Newton closure gives 106.689319802 in, a difference of -1.6030e-3 in. Where does that gap come from?', '["The two use different front arm lengths, the oracle working from the rounded 106.6667 in and the engine from the exact value behind it.", "The oracle solves the closure by Newton iteration and stops at its own tolerance, while the engine''s intersection is exact.", "The engine reports the stroke of the equalizer bearing and the oracle reports the stroke at the polished rod, which the front arm separates.", "The engine walks 360 crank steps and takes the extremes of what it sampled, so it can only find a turning point a whole degree lands on."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The bottom of the polished rod stroke lands at 359.000000000 deg, sample index 359 of 360, and a gap of -1.6030e-3 in is what one degree of sampling costs.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 4, 'The engine reports an upstroke fraction of 0.544444444444 and the oracle 0.547222222222, a difference of -2.7778e-3. What is that difference?', '["The time the polished rod spends stationary at the top of the stroke, which the oracle counts into the upstroke and the engine does not.", "One sample in 360, the same discretisation that moves the stroke, rather than a disagreement about the geometry.", "A real difference in the two closures, since implicit differentiation gives the oracle a turning point the engine''s sampled extremes cannot reach.", "The upstroke fraction at the equalizer bearing against the one at the polished rod."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Either way the upstroke takes 54.444444444 percent of the turn, and both numbers come from sampling one revolution rather than from any dimension.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 5, 'At 10 spm the published unit takes 3.266666667 s going up and 2.733333333 s coming down, on a crank turning at one constant speed. How?', '["The gearbox slows the crank through the upstroke because the load is higher there, and the two durations record that change of speed.", "The upstroke is longer because the rod string stretches under the fluid load, and the polished rod has to travel all of that stretch before the plunger lifts at all.", "The four-bar returns the polished rod to the bottom of its travel in less than half a revolution, so the two halves of the stroke take different times.", "The pitman is in compression on the way down and in tension on the way up, and the difference in the two durations is the play that takes up."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Nothing was asked for and the shape delivered it: the upstroke is 54.444444444 percent of the revolution and the downstroke is the rest.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 6, 'A designer takes the stroke as twice the crank radius. What does the published unit say about that?', '["The ratio there is 3.704434612, and it is not even a constant of the machine: 3.497252348 at a crank radius of 20.000 in and 3.921849154 at 32.000 in.", "The ratio is 3.704434612, and since it holds across the crank sweep a stroke scaled from one crank radius to another is safe.", "The rule is right for the equalizer bearing, whose travel is twice the crank radius, and the beam then scales it by the arm ratio.", "The rule fails only near the closure limit, so it can be relied on below a crank radius of 32.000 in and not above it."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Bigger cranks give more stroke per inch of crank: the last column drifts by more than a tenth of itself from 20.000 in to 32.000 in of crank radius.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 7, 'Walk the front arm from 80.0000 in to 90.0000, 100.0000, 106.6667, 110.0000 and 120.0000 in. What happens?', '["The stroke over the front arm drifts as the crank ratio does, from 1.000197032783 at 106.6667 in outward toward the ends of the sweep.", "The stroke scales exactly but the upstroke fraction moves with it, from 0.519444444 at the short arm to 0.569444444 at the long one.", "The linkage stops closing beyond 120.0000 in, because a front arm that long cannot be reached by a pitman of 80 in.", "The stroke over the front arm reads 1.000197032783 at every one of them, and the upstroke fraction stays at 0.544444444 throughout."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The front arm is a pure lever ratio: it changes the size of the motion and nothing about its shape or its timing, which is why 80.0000 in gives 80.015762623 in of stroke.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 8, 'Which quantity is owned by no single dimension of the unit?', '["The largest torque factor, which is 56.305306799 in on the published unit and follows no one length in the sweeps.", "The upstroke fraction, which moved only from 0.519444444 to 0.569444444 while the crank nearly doubled the stroke and did not move at all under the front arm.", "The stroke, since the crank and the front arm both move it and neither of them owns it on its own.", "The closure residual, which is at round-off on the published unit and belongs to the arithmetic rather than to any dimension."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Timing is a property of the whole shape, which the generic geometry makes plain by returning 0.544444444 at requested strokes of 54.0, 74.0, 100.0, 120.0 and 144.0 in alike.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 9, 'The largest torque factor on the published unit is 56.305306799 in. What has to be supplied before it becomes a torque, and what before it becomes a velocity?', '["A load for the torque and a pumping speed for the velocity, and the closure supplies neither one.", "A load for the torque and a load for the velocity as well, since the polished rod moves only as fast as the string under it allows.", "A gearbox rating for the torque, such as the 320000 in-lb a C-320D-200-100 carries, and a pumping speed for the velocity.", "Nothing for the torque, the factor already being in in-lb, and a pumping speed for the velocity, which is the only place time enters."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The factor is in inches, so a torque needs a load the closure never names, and velocity is that factor times the crank''s angular velocity, which is where the spm arrives.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 10, 'At 10 spm the fastest downward velocity on the published unit is 58.962779400 in/s and the fastest upward is -52.143013743 in/s. What does that pair show?', '["That the rod string is loading the upstroke, so the polished rod cannot rise as fast as it falls.", "That the crank slows through the upstroke, since a constant speed crank driving a symmetric linkage would give the same speed each way.", "That the two halves are not mirror images, at a ratio of 0.884337785 that no dimension was chosen to produce.", "That the sampling at 15 deg steps missed the true upward extreme, which lies between two samples and would match the downward one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The closure knows six lengths and nothing about a well, so the asymmetry is geometry: at 10 spm the ratio of the slower to the faster is 0.884337785.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 11, 'Ask the generic geometry for a stroke of 100.0 in. What do you get?', '["100.0 in exactly, the generic geometry solving for the dimensions that give it.", "99.998422475 in, from a fixed shape scaled to the request, with a note saying it is not a manufacturer''s unit.", "A refusal, because the package ships no named unit dimensions and a stroke on its own is not a geometry it can close.", "99.998422475 in with an upstroke fraction scaled to match, since a shape stretched to a new stroke has its timing stretched with it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The generic route achieves 53.999148136 in for 54.0 and 143.997728363 in for 144.0 in the same way, and it returns an upstroke fraction of 0.544444444 every time.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 12, 'Why is assuming the polished rod moves as a sine worse than it looks?', '["A sine has no upstroke fraction at all, so nothing in the four-bar''s 0.544444444444 can be recovered from it.", "A sine and the four-bar disagree by the whole 106.687716837 in of stroke, which is what the raw comparison between the two curves returns.", "A sine reaches its extreme at the crank angle where the four-bar reaches its fastest velocity, so the two are out by a quarter of a turn.", "The four-bar is still 1.876427807 in below the top at cycle fraction 0.500000, where a simple harmonic stroke of the same length is at its extreme."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The four-bar turns at cycle fraction 0.541667, where its position reads 0.003849789 in, and every load a rod string sees is an acceleration times a mass.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 13, 'The published unit closes at a crank radius of 32.000 in and gives 125.499172938 in of stroke. At 34.000 in, what comes back?', '["No stroke at all, only the message that the pitman cannot reach the beam.", "A stroke computed from the crank angles where the linkage does close, with the rest of the revolution left out and a warning attached.", "A stroke with the beam angle clamped at the limit of closure, which is what makes the ratio of stroke to crank radius drift upward.", "A refusal, and with it the upstroke fraction and the largest torque factor, since those two do not depend on closing at every crank angle."]'::jsonb, 0, 'There is no clamped beam angle and no partial answer: the engine reports that the geometry does not close and names the crank radius, the pitman and the crankshaft position as what to check.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 14, 'What does the designation C-320D-200-100 tell a designer, and what does it not?', '["The three numbers a design is checked against and the front arm, the rear arm and the pitman that produce the stroke it names.", "A conventional unit with a double reduction gearbox, from which the six lengths follow once the stroke of 100 in is known.", "A gearbox rating of 320000 in-lb, a structural capacity of 20000 lb and a stroke of 100 in, and not one dimension of the linkage.", "Nothing a design can use, since a designation is a name and the package parses it only to reject strings that are not designations."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The designation carries ratings, so M-228D-173-86 is a Mark II at 228000 in-lb, 17300 lb and 86 in, and the package ships no named unit dimensions at all.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm04-the-linkage', 15, 'The published linkage gives 106.687716837 in of travel. Why is sizing a unit on that number alone a mistake?', '["Because the linkage reports the stroke of the equalizer bearing rather than of the polished rod, and the front arm has still to be applied.", "Because a 1.7500 in plunger against 1950.0 psi carries 4690.299657039 lb, and that load stretches the published taper 17.560655738 in before the plunger moves.", "Because the stroke is sampled at 360 crank steps and is low by -1.6030e-3 in, so a unit chosen on it is marginally undersized.", "Because the stroke moves with the crank radius, and a unit is bought on its designation stroke rather than on the one the closure returns."]'::jsonb, 1, 'No linkage dimension appears anywhere in that 17.560655738 in: it belongs to the string and the load, so the surface stroke and the pump''s stroke are two different quantities.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 1, 'A 1.75 in plunger pulls 962.112750162 lb against 400.0 psi of differential and 1924.225500324 lb against 800.0 psi. What does the stretch column do over the same step?', '["It doubles as well, but only while the load stays below the string''s buoyed weight, above which stretch outruns load.", "It rises by less than double, since the string stiffens as it is loaded and each extra pound of fluid load buys less stretch than the pound before it.", "It is unchanged, the static stretch being a property of the string''s spring rate of 267.091373300 lb/in rather than of the load hanging on it.", "It doubles too, from 3.602185792 in to 7.204371585 in, because a fluid load and the stretch it causes are the same product read twice."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Fluid load is a differential times an area, exactly linear in both, so at 1950.0 psi the load is 4690.299657039 lb and the stretch is 17.560655738 in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 2, 'Which of these is inside the fluid load calculation?', '["Nothing but a pressure difference and a plunger area.", "The depth of the pump, since a taller column of fluid is heavier and the plunger has to carry all of it.", "The buoyancy factor, which is applied to the column standing on the plunger just as it is to the rods.", "The fluid friction on the plunger, which the engine adds as a fraction of the differential before the area is applied."]'::jsonb, 0, 'There is no third term: 1950.0 psi across 2.405281875 in2 is 4690.299657039 lb, and no depth, rod weight, gradient or friction appears anywhere in it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 3, 'The differential is a discharge pressure minus an intake pressure, both psia. What happens if a design mixes an absolute discharge with a gauge intake?', '["The engine catches it, since a gauge intake makes the discharge fail to exceed the intake and the refusal fires.", "Nothing, because the two pressures are subtracted and a constant offset in either one cancels in the difference.", "The differential comes out wrong by atmospheric pressure and the engine cannot notice, because a differential is all it ever sees.", "The fluid load is wrong but the rated displacement is not, since a displacement carries no pressure term and is computed from the geometry."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both pressures go in and only their difference comes out, so 1950.0 psi is accepted as a differential without the engine ever seeing what it was built from.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 4, 'Set the discharge below the intake. What does the engine return?', '["A fluid load of 0.000000 lb with a warning code, since a plunger with nothing to lift is a design error rather than a parse error.", "ok false with a message about the fluid level, the tubing pressure and the inflow, rather than a harmless zero.", "A negative fluid load, the arithmetic answer, left for the caller to read as a plunger held down.", "ok true with the fluid load clamped at 0.000000 lb, which is what lets the rest of the design continue to a rated displacement."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fluid load that would have been reported reads 0.000000 lb, and this is the only physical check anywhere in the pump calculation.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 5, 'The plunger sizes on the catalogue list are evenly spaced. Why are the loads not?', '["They move as the square of the diameter, so the step from 2.5000 in to 2.7500 in adds more load than the whole 1.0625 in plunger carries.", "They move as the square of the diameter only above 2.0000 in, below which the area and the diameter are close enough to track each other.", "The differential of 1950.0 psi is held fixed across the list, and a fixed differential makes every load on it grow faster than linearly.", "The published rod weights include couplings, and the coupling allowance of 1.087 falls unevenly across the sizes the plungers are paired with."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The area goes as the square: 4.908738521 in2 carries 9572.040116 lb and 5.939573611 in2 carries 11582.168541 lb, while a 1.0625 in plunger carries 1728.949746 lb in total.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 6, 'The engine''s PUMP_CONSTANT is 0.116571155977. What does it multiply?', '["The plunger area, which is what the units of bbl per day per in2 per in per spm are naming.", "The diameter squared for a rating and the area for a volume per stroke, the two forms differing by the pi over four between them.", "The swept volume in in3, which the constant then converts through the 9702 in3 per bbl it is built from.", "The diameter squared, because pi over four is already inside the constant."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Feeding it the area applies pi over four a second time and understates the displacement by 21.460184 percent, taking a 1.7500 in plunger from 380.874258458 to 299.137943078 bbl/d.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 7, 'The predecessor''s area form understates the rated displacement by 21.460184 percent at every plunger size from 1.0625 in to 2.7500 in. Why does that constancy make it worse?', '["Because a constant percentage cannot be recovered once a design is complete, while an error that varies can be fitted out of the results.", "A shortfall that changed with size would look like a bug, and one that holds everywhere reads as a conservative design basis instead.", "Because the fluid load is understated by the same fraction, so the load and the rate still stand in the right ratio.", "Because the percentage is exactly pi over four, which a reader is likely to recognise and dismiss as a rounding of the constant."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It holds from 140.398801906 against 110.268961160 bbl/d at the small end to 940.526230069 against 738.687573723 bbl/d at the large end, and it never once changes.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 8, 'A design needs more volume, so the plunger goes from 1.7500 in to 2.5000 in. What comes with the extra rate?', '["Nothing, because the rated displacement rises with the area while the fluid load rises with the differential, and the differential was not changed.", "A longer plunger stroke, since a wider plunger transfers the load sooner and the string spends less of the surface stroke stretching.", "The fluid load goes from 4690.299657 lb to 9572.040116 lb, since both the volume and the load come off the same square.", "A lower load per barrel, because the rating rises from 380.874258458 to 777.294405015 bbl/d while the load rises only with the diameter."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Load and displacement come off the same area, so no plunger size gives more volume and less pull, and every extra pound lands on the same rod string.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 9, 'What does the plunger area calculation refuse?', '["Nothing at all, since it will square any diameter handed to it, including one larger than the tubing it is meant to sit in.", "A plunger larger than the tubing, which is the one geometric check the pump side of the engine carries.", "A diameter that is not on the catalogue list, the eight sizes from 1.0625 in to 2.7500 in being the whole of what it knows.", "A plunger with no differential to lift against, which is where the area calculation reports its only refusal."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The barrel, the tubing and the pump bore are not modelled anywhere, so a 2.7500 in plunger returns an area of 5.939573611 in2 like any other and the only limit is the one a designer brings.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 10, 'A rated displacement is exactly proportional to the pumping speed. Why can a designer not simply double the barrels by doubling the speed?', '["Because the pump constant of 0.116571155977 is fixed at 10 spm, so a rating quoted at any other speed has to be rescaled by the ratio of the two speeds before it means anything.", "Because the volume swept per stroke falls as the speed rises, the barrel having less time to fill at each stroke, so a doubled speed buys well under a doubled rate.", "Because doubling the speed halves the fillage, which drops it below 0.85, and incompleteFillage then recomputes the rating against that threshold.", "Because the joining function refuses any design at or above the string''s own fundamental, and a rating was computed without ever knowing a rod string was there."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A rating is a constant, a diameter squared, a stroke and a speed, and not one of the four knows the rods exist: ODUMA-4 is refused at 60 spm against a fundamental of 59.134268422 spm.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 11, 'A 1.7500 in plunger sweeps 0.026449601 bbl per stroke and the engine reports 380.874258458 bbl/d at 10 spm. What happens between those two numbers?', '["The fillage and the pump efficiency are applied, which is what turns a swept volume into the rate a design sheet carries.", "The plunger stroke replaces the surface stroke, since a rating over a day has to use the travel the plunger actually made.", "Nothing but counting strokes, which is why the volume per stroke carries no speed in it and the rating carries speed only through the count.", "The 9702 in3 per bbl conversion, which is where the swept 256.614032 in3 becomes a volume the rating can be quoted in."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The rating is that volume repeated, so a rated displacement contains no dynamics whatever and the volume per stroke does not depend on speed at all.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 12, 'Which term does the rated displacement carry?', '["The differential, which is what fixes the fluid load, and through the fluid load the stretch that shortens the stroke.", "None of the fluid, the depth, the string or the differential the pump works against.", "The fluid specific gravity, which enters through the buoyancy factor of 0.872611464968 applied to the swept volume.", "The pump efficiency, which the caller types in and which the rating applies before it is reported in bbl/d."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A constant, a diameter squared, a stroke and a speed are the four factors, so doubling the speed doubles the rating and doubling the stroke doubles it too.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 13, 'A rated displacement of 380.874258458 bbl/d is quoted on a design sheet. What two things have been assumed?', '["That the barrel fills on every stroke and that the plunger travels the whole 106.687717 in of surface stroke.", "That the barrel fills on every stroke and that the fillage is at or above 0.85, which is where incompleteFillage stops firing.", "That the plunger travels the surface stroke and that the pump efficiency typed in is right.", "That the inflow can supply the rate and that the differential of 1950.0 psi is available at the pump for the whole day."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Both are conditions on the well and neither is an input to the multiplication, so neither one can be violated by it: 380.874258458 bbl/d was computed on a length the pump never sees.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 14, 'A 2.5000 in plunger sweeps 0.053978778 bbl of space per stroke. What does that number tell you about what arrives at the tank?', '["Nothing directly, but multiplying by the fillage of 0.85 gives the produced volume, which is the step the rating leaves out.", "That 0.053978778 bbl is an upper bound short by the difference between the two strokes.", "Nothing, because the figure is identical whether the space fills with liquid, with gas or with nothing at all.", "That the tank sees 0.053978778 bbl per stroke less the pump efficiency, which is the only loss the swept volume does not already carry."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A swept volume is a geometric space, and the engine carries a separate fillage input and a separate pump efficiency input precisely because it cannot express either one.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm05-the-pump-itself', 15, 'The engine refuses a plunger with no differential and says nothing about a rating no well could reach. What is that asymmetry?', '["A gap that incompleteFillage closes, since it fires below 0.85 and the rating is recomputed once the caller supplies the fillage.", "A hard check on the one condition that makes the arithmetic meaningless, and no check at all on the two that make the answer wrong.", "A deliberate ordering, the refusals being raised first and the warnings raised only after every number has been computed.", "An oversight in the pump module, since the same package refuses a speed at or above the fundamental for exactly this reason."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The same engine carries a warning code for the condition the rating denies, and the rating simply does not consult it: fillage is a separate number a caller types in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 1, 'The order of work puts the string first and the speed only after the note has been read. Why that way round?', '["Because the fluid load has to be known before the string can be built, and only the string can supply the buoyancy factor the load needs.", "Because the design is refused at or above the string''s own fundamental, so a speed chosen before the note is a speed that may not be allowed.", "Because the linkage stroke is an input to the compliance sum, so the four-bar has to be closed before a spring rate can be got at all.", "Because the note moves with the pumping speed, and reading it after a speed is chosen would give a fundamental for that speed only."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A design at 60 spm on a string whose fundamental is 59.134268422 spm comes back ok false with one error and nothing else, so the note is worth having early.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 2, 'Which check on a spring rate is free, and what does it catch?', '["That the spring rate must exceed the softest section''s own rate, which catches a taper given in the wrong order.", "That the elastic constant and the spring rate must multiply to one, which catches a compliance inverted at the wrong step.", "That the spring rate must fall between the two section rates, which catches a length entered against the wrong section.", "That a string must be softer than any section in it, which catches spring rates added where compliances should have been."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The published taper''s softest section alone would give 509.452063888 lb/in and the string comes out at 267.091373300 lb/in, while adding rates gives 1070.889032254 lb/in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 3, 'Which identity can be run on a 1.7500 in plunger without a solver?', '["2.405281875 in2 at 1950.0 psi gives 4690.299657039 lb, and that plunger over 106.687717 in sweeps 0.026449601 bbl, reading 380.874258458 bbl/d at 10 spm.", "That 380.874258458 bbl/d divided by 10 spm and by the surface stroke returns the plunger area of 2.405281875 in2 the load was built on.", "That the fluid load of 4690.299657039 lb divided by the spring rate returns the plunger stroke the pump actually travels.", "That the swept 0.026449601 bbl times the fillage of 0.9 returns the production, which is the check the rating is quoted against."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The load is a differential times an area and the rating is a constant times a diameter squared times a stroke times a speed, and both can be done by hand.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 4, 'A worked string comes back at 1070.889032254 lb/in. What went wrong, and by how much?', '["The buoyancy factor was applied to the compliance rather than to the weight, which stiffens the string by the fraction the fluid carries.", "The two sections were entered in the wrong order, which is the taperStepsUp condition and returns 251.236634246 lb/in instead.", "The section spring rates were added rather than the compliances, and the answer is 4.009448 times the true rate of 267.091373300 lb/in.", "The elastic constant was used in place of the spring rate, so the answer is the reciprocal of 3.744037060e-3 in/lb taken twice."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A series string is softer than its softest section, and 1070.889032254 lb/in stands above even the 561.436968366 lb/in of the 3/4 section on its own.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 5, 'A buoyancy factor of 0.847133757962 turns up in a worked design at specific gravity 1.00. What has been used, and what does it cost?', '["The factor for a heavier fluid, since 0.847133757962 is what specific gravity 1.15 gives and the fluid was entered at the wrong density.", "The predecessor''s factor with a 1.2 in it, which removes 253.248408 lb the polished rod is actually carrying.", "The factor with steel taken at a specific gravity other than 7.85, which changes the divisor rather than the numerator of the expression.", "The correct factor, since 0.872611464968 is the value before the coupling allowance of 1.087 has been taken out of the rod weights."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The true factor is one minus the fluid specific gravity over 7.85, giving 0.872611464968 and a buoyed weight of 8673.757961783 lb on the published taper.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 6, 'Why is twice the crank radius a trap rather than a rough rule?', '["The published unit''s ratio is 3.704434612 and it drifts to 3.921849154 at a crank radius of 32.000 in, so it is not even a constant of the machine.", "Because the ratio holds only while the linkage closes, and above a crank radius of 32.000 in there is no stroke to take a ratio of.", "Because it gives the travel of the equalizer bearing, which the front arm then scales into the polished rod stroke.", "Because the crank radius is not one of the six lengths the closure takes, so the ratio is built on a dimension the unit does not have."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The stroke is the beam sweep times the front arm, 1.000197032783 rad by 106.6667 in, and nothing in the unit is twice the crank radius.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 7, 'Six quantities are settled before a march: weight in air, buoyed weight, spring rate, fundamental, surface stroke and fluid load. How many depend on the pumping speed?', '["The fundamental alone, since it is a frequency and every frequency is measured against the same clock the speed is.", "The fundamental and the fluid load, the second because the differential the pump works against rises with how fast it is worked.", "The surface stroke alone, since the four-bar is sampled over one revolution and a faster revolution is sampled at a different resolution.", "None, which is why this tier can be graded before any card exists."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A compliance sum, a buoyancy factor, an eigenvalue, a four-bar closure and a differential times an area are the same at 5 spm and at 15 spm.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 8, 'Step three of the order of work reads the warnings the string raises. What does that step actually separate?', '["A condition the oracle gates at 2 percent from one it gates at 3 percent, which is what decides whether a code is raised.", "A warning raised by the string from one raised by the pump.", "A design error the arithmetic can still be carried out for from an input that cannot be read at all.", "A condition the caller can fix from one the engine repairs on its own, the reversed taper being sorted into order before the sum."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The reversed taper returns ok true with taperStepsUp and 251.236634246 lb/in, while a rod size of seven eighths returns ok false with one error and no numbers.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 9, 'Step five closes the linkage. Which three numbers does it leave a design holding?', '["A stroke of 106.687716837 in, a beam sweep of 1.000197032783 rad and a gearbox rating of 320000 in-lb.", "A stroke of 106.687716837 in, an upstroke fraction of 0.544444444444 and a largest torque factor of 56.305306799 in.", "A stroke of 106.687716837 in, a fluid load of 4690.299657039 lb and a rated displacement over it of 380.874258458 bbl/d at 10 spm.", "A stroke of 106.687716837 in, a fastest velocity of 58.962779400 in/s and a structural capacity of 20000 lb."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The torque factor is in inches, so it is half of a torque and the closure supplies no load, and the ratings a designation carries are checked against rather than computed.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 10, 'The spring rule on the published taper takes a surface stroke of 64.000000 in, a fluid load of 5000.000000 lb and an elastic constant of 3.744037060e-3 in/lb. What does it give?', '["45.279814701 in, from subtracting 18.720185299 in of static stretch, and it does not depend on speed.", "45.279814701 in at low speed only, the static stretch growing with speed.", "18.720185299 in, which is the plunger''s travel, the surface stroke being what the polished rod does rather than the pump.", "45.279814701 in, and it is the travel the barrel actually sees, since the plunger cannot move until the stretch is taken up."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The rule is a stroke minus a stretch and nothing else, so 64.000000 in less 18.720185299 in gives 45.279814701 in at every speed alike.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 11, 'The wave march gives 45.286791250 in at 0.5 spm and 53.042713176 in at 15.0 spm against that same spring rule. What are those two rows for?', '["The slow row shows the march failing to settle, and the fast row shows it settled, so only the second is quoted.", "The two rows bracket the working range, so a design between them can be read off by interpolating the overtravel between the two figures.", "The slow row shows the wave answer landing on the spring rule at 0.015408 percent, and the fast row shows it 17.144281 percent above.", "The slow row is the spring rule recomputed by the march, and the fast row is the same rule with the inertia term the static form leaves out."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The wave answer is longer at both ends, and the extra travel is inertia: the string is still moving when the polished rod turns round.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 12, 'Three consecutive speeds on that ladder show the overtravel rising. What may be concluded?', '["That the overtravel grows with speed, which the two ends of the ladder confirm and the middle rows illustrate.", "That the march has settled there, a smooth rise being what a converged solver gives.", "That the design should be run at the lowest of the three, the overtravel being smallest there and the stroke nearest the spring rule.", "Nothing, because single speeds step back below the speed before them and three rising rows are not a trend."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The settled cycle a march lands on depends on where the valves transfer relative to the wave returning from the pump, which is not a smooth function of speed.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 13, 'At 0.5 spm the published taper gives a peak of 13756.379902 lb and a minimum of 8586.448887 lb. What are those two numbers standing beside?', '["The peak and minimum the same string gives at 15.0 spm, 18752.731892 lb and 3651.294230 lb, with nothing but the speed changed between them.", "A buoyed weight plus fluid load of 13673.757962 lb and a buoyed weight of 8673.757962 lb, which is what a rod string does when it is only a spring.", "The structural capacity of the unit and the buoyed weight, which is the pair the structural check is made against.", "The spring rule of 45.279814701 in and the wave march of 45.286791250 in, converted into loads through the spring rate."]'::jsonb, 1, 'At the slow end both loads land where a spring says they should, and everything the course calls dynamic is the distance from that row to the row a real unit runs at.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 14, 'At 5 spm the march takes 11728 steps in a cycle and the card the loads are read from has 181 points. Which number do the reported loads come off?', '["The 181, because the card is decimated before the two loads are read from it.", "The 11728, since the loads are accumulated over every marched step and only the plotted card is thinned.", "Both, the peak coming from the marched steps and the minimum from the card, which is why the two are not comparable.", "The 181 for the plot and the 11728 for the reported pair, which is what keeps the design''s numbers independent of the drawing."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The decimation stride at 5 spm is 65, so 181 points survive out of 11728, and what the Expert tier asks is how much of a reported load is an artefact of that.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'module', 'm06-the-associate-reading', 15, 'What does the Associate tier leave unanswered?', '["What the string weighs in fluid, which needs the fluid load and therefore the pump to have been sized first.", "What the linkage stroke is, which cannot be settled until a rod string has been hung under the polished rod.", "What the plunger travels and what the well makes, both of which wait on a card.", "What the fundamental is, since a note has meaning only against a speed and no speed has been chosen at this stage."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fluid load that drives the pump has already stretched the published taper 17.560655738 in before the plunger has moved, and that gap is a card result.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 1, 'Which of the four objects owns a polished rod horsepower, and what does it need that the other three never ask for?', '["The linkage, since power is a torque times an angular velocity and the torque factor of 56.305306799 in is where a torque begins.", "The pump, since power is what moves a rated displacement of 380.874258458 bbl/d against the differential the plunger works on.", "The card, and it needs a pumping speed, which no closed form in the string, the linkage or the pump takes.", "The string, because the power delivered at the polished rod is the buoyed weight of 8673.757961783 lb lifted through the stroke each cycle."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The card owns the plunger stroke, both polished rod loads, the card area, the horsepower and every warning about them, and every one of those needs a march.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 2, 'Nothing in the string, the linkage or the pump needs a march and everything in the card does. What does that seam decide?', '["Where the three tiers of the course are cut, since a compliance sum, an eigenvalue, a four-bar closure and a differential times an area are the same at 5 spm and at 15 spm.", "Which numbers the oracle can check, since only a closed form has a second route to compare against and a march has none.", "Which numbers a studio can compute quickly, the closed forms being cheap and the march expensive, so the tiers follow the cost.", "Which numbers carry warnings, since a warning code can only be raised about a quantity that was solved rather than evaluated."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Everything the card owns is different at every speed and different again at a different node count, and that is why the Associate tier stops where it does.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 3, 'A design sheet carries 380.874258458 bbl/d as the well''s rate. Which object''s number is that, and what has been left out?', '["The card''s, since a displacement is a production and only a solved card can report what the well made in a day.", "The linkage''s, since the 106.687717 in standing inside that product is a four-bar closure result and the pump only scales it by an area.", "The string''s, since the 17.560655738 in of stretch is already inside the figure and that is what makes it a well rate.", "The pump''s, computed on the linkage''s surface stroke, and how much shorter the plunger''s own travel is belongs to the card."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A rating assumes a full barrel and the whole 106.687717 in of surface stroke, and neither assumption is an input to the multiplication that produced it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 4, 'Three closed forms on the published taper land on the oracle with a difference of 0.000e+0 while its note lands 7.7207e-5 spm away. What separates those two kinds of agreement?', '["The closed forms are carried in double precision and the eigenvalue in single, which is where the seventh figure of the note is lost.", "A compliance sum and Archimedes have exactly one answer, so two routes to them must agree bit for bit, while an eigenvalue is found by searching and two searches land near each other.", "The closed forms and the oracle share a source, so their agreement is a check on the arithmetic rather than on the physics, while the note is the one quantity the two routes reach independently of each other.", "The note is a measured property of the string while the other three are definitions, so only the note carries the uncertainty of the catalogue weights behind it."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The engine scan and a dense two million point mode scan agree to every figure at 53.362124006 spm, so what is left against the oracle is two numerical routes to one eigenvalue.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 5, 'A design comes back with no warnings at all. What is the mistake a reader is about to make?', '["Reading silence as approval, when the design was asked the questions this engine asks and was never asked about buckling, gas or wear.", "Assuming the warnings were suppressed, when in fact every code is raised only once the march has settled into a repeating cycle.", "Assuming the design is inside the oracle''s tolerances, when the gates hold the two routes only to 2 percent on plunger stroke.", "Assuming the numbers converged, when a silent return says nothing about whether the node count was fine enough to resolve them."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Rod buckling, tubing movement, fluid friction, gas interference, side loading and rod on tubing wear are not modelled, so no number in the output speaks to any of them.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 6, 'predictCard marches the damped wave equation and diagnoseCard propagates Fourier harmonics of a measured card. What does sharing no code path buy?', '["Speed, since a diagnostic that reused the march would have to run the march before it could read anything at all.", "That the measured card stays the authority and the marched card the estimate, the two being kept apart so the approximation cannot contaminate the measurement.", "That either can be used to check the other, since a march down the string and harmonics propagated up are independent routes.", "Nothing beyond tidiness, since a caller simply picks whichever of the two matches the data that happens to be on hand."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The two solvers sit in the same file and share no code path, which is the same discipline the goldens use in taking four different routes on purpose.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 7, 'The published taper''s sections have compliances of 1.962893216e-3 and 1.781143844e-3 in/lb. What does the engine do with them?', '["Adds the section spring rates of 509.452063888 and 561.436968366 lb/in, giving 1070.889032254 lb/in for the string.", "Adds them to 3.744037060e-3 in/lb and inverts, giving 267.091373300 lb/in.", "Weights each compliance by its section length over the total 5000 ft before summing, so the longer section counts for more.", "Takes the larger of the two compliances, since a series string can be no stiffer than its softest section allows it to be."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Compliances add and stiffnesses do not, and adding the rates instead gives an answer 4.009448 times the true one.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 8, 'What is inside the buoyancy factor of 0.872611464968?', '["The fluid specific gravity over 7.85, together with the coupling allowance of 1.087 that the published rod weights carry.", "One minus 1.2 times the fluid specific gravity over 7.85, which is the form that gives 0.847133757962 at specific gravity 1.00.", "One minus the fluid specific gravity over the steel density of 490 lb/ft3, which is where the 7.85 in the expression comes from.", "One minus the fluid specific gravity over 7.85, and no other coefficient at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Archimedes and nothing else: the same factor takes 9940.000000 lb to 8673.757961783 lb and 13344.000000 lb to 11644.127388535 lb.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 9, 'The split with the highest note in the 5000 ft sweep rings at 53.642386647 spm at 2500 ft of 7/8, and the stiffest split is the all 7/8 string at 305.671238333 lb/in. What does that pair rule out?', '["Reading a fundamental off a spring rate, since the note turns and comes back while the stiffness climbs the whole way.", "Reading a spring rate off a fundamental, though the reverse still holds because a stiffer string does ring higher until the sweep turns.", "Using the split sweep at all, since two columns that disagree about which row is best cannot both be describing the same string.", "Comparing splits of different length, the trap the uniform string already showed at 3404.000000 lb."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The published taper at 267.091373300 lb/in rings at 53.362124006 spm, below the softer 2500 ft split, and the stiffest string of all rings lowest at 48.866282953 spm.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 10, 'A 7/8 rod is published at 2.224000 lb/ft and its bare steel weight is 2.046159929 lb/ft. Why does the catalog carry the larger figure?', '["Because the published figure is a design weight carrying a service factor, and the bare steel figure is the physical one.", "Because a published rod weight includes the couplings, so a weight built from area and density is short by that gap on every foot.", "Because the published figure is quoted in fluid and the bare steel figure in air, the difference being the buoyancy the rod sits in.", "Because the published figure is the weight of the rod plus the fluid displaced inside the tubing it hangs in."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Published weight over bare steel is 1.086914062177 on a 7/8 rod, near the stated coupling allowance of 1.087 without matching it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 11, 'The taper is 3404.000000 lb lighter in air and 12.365341 lb/in stiffer than the published uniform string. What is wrong with quoting that as what a taper buys?', '["The two figures are 25.509592 and 4.854369 percent, and percentages taken on different quantities cannot be compared with each other.", "The comparison uses weight in air, and a taper''s advantage only appears once both strings are put in fluid of specific gravity 1.00.", "The uniform string is 6000 ft and the taper is 5000 ft, so most of both gaps is the thousand feet rather than the taper.", "The uniform string is the published case and the taper is not, so only one of the two carries an oracle behind it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The honest same length comparison is the split sweep, which holds 5000 ft fixed and walks the spring rate from 224.574787346 to 305.671238333 lb/in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 12, 'The catalog''s coupling allowance of 1.087 shows up twice, in a rod weight and in a wave speed. How does it reach the wave speed?', '["It is applied directly to the acoustic velocity, which is why the catalog also ships a single conventional figure of 16333 ft/s.", "It raises the mass and the stiffness together, so the velocity is unchanged and only the string''s weight in air moves.", "It enters through Young''s modulus of 30500000 psi, which the catalog reduces to account for the couplings between rods.", "The extra mass per foot is not matched by extra area, so the bare steel 16981.875572480 ft/s falls to 16288.760984482 ft/s on a 7/8 rod."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Dividing the bare steel velocity by the square root of 1.087 gives 16288.117080010 ft/s, and the wave speed is what a fundamental is built from.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 13, 'Reverse the published taper, putting 3/4 above 7/8 over the same 5000 ft. What comes back, and why that treatment?', '["ok true with a taperStepsUp warning and 251.236634246 lb/in, since a reversed order is a design error rather than a parse error.", "ok false with one error, since a section cannot carry what hangs below it and the string as given is not buildable.", "ok true with no warning at all and 267.091373300 lb/in, the compliance sum being indifferent to the order in which the sections happen to arrive.", "ok true with a taperStepsUp warning and 267.091373300 lb/in, the code being raised after the sum has been taken."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The compliance sum really is indifferent to order, and the string is not, so the engine computes the answer and says so with a code.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 14, 'Ask for a rod size of seven eighths and the engine refuses. What was the alternative?', '["A default to the nearest catalog size, which would have continued the design on 7/8 rods with a note attached to the answer.", "A bare numeric parse reading 7/8 as 7.8 in, an area of 47.783624 in2 against the true 0.601320469 in2, a factor of 79.464490.", "A refusal from the compliance sum instead, once a section with no readable area reached it and left the sum with nothing to add.", "An area of 0.601320469 in2 anyway, since the catalog carries spelled out names beside the fractions for exactly this case."]'::jsonb, 1, 'A string with eighty times its true area barely stretches, so every load and stroke after it would have been confidently wrong with nothing to flag it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 15, 'A design needs more barrels a day. One lever is the plunger size and one is the pumping speed. Which of the two does the string''s own note put a hard stop on?', '["The plunger, since a wider plunger hangs a larger fluid load on the string and a string''s note falls as the load it carries rises.", "Both, since a rated displacement is a diameter squared times a stroke times a speed and the refusal is written against that product rather than against the speed alone.", "The speed, since the engine refuses any design at or above the fundamental, which on ODUMA-4 is 59.134268421585 spm, and nothing about a plunger enters that eigenvalue.", "Neither, since a design near the note is warned about rather than refused, the engine returning an answer with a code beside it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The fundamental is a stepped bar eigenvalue in the string''s own lengths, areas and weights, so going to a 2.5000 in plunger takes the rating from 380.874258458 to 777.294405015 bbl/d and leaves the note alone.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 16, 'The uniform string gives mode ratios of 3.000000000 and 5.000000000 and the published taper gives 2.693424993 and 4.578723505. What does the difference say?', '["That the taper''s modes were found on a coarser grid, since the engine scan lays fewer points near the higher roots.", "That the taper factor of 1.092008382351 multiplies the fundamental and leaves the higher modes where a uniform bar puts them.", "That the uniform case is the fitted one, its ratios having been tuned to the odd harmonics a fixed free bar is expected to give.", "That a stepped bar is not a uniform bar, and how far the ratios miss the odd harmonics is what a taper factor measures."]'::jsonb, 3, 'One material and one area give a closed form and the numerics land on it, while the taper''s modes at 143.726878480 and 244.330411470 spm land nowhere near three and five.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 17, 'The engine reports a base note of 48.866038821915 spm for the published taper. What is that number?', '["The note of the all 3/4 string of the same length, which the taper factor corrects upward for the 7/8 section above it.", "The uniform quarter wave the string would have if it were all one size, which a taper factor of 1.092008382351 then multiplies.", "The lowest note in the split sweep, at 48.865672625 spm, which every stepped string of that length is scaled from.", "The note the scan bisects to before the higher modes are removed, the taper factor then being reported beside it as a diagnostic."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The base and the factor multiply to the 53.362124006 spm the engine returns, and ODUMA-4 goes the same way from 50.904388515803 spm at a factor of 1.161673288802.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 18, 'The natural frequency scan means to lay 400 evenly spaced points from 2.443302 to 195.464155 spm. How many land inside that range?', '["28 of the 401, because the increment is added to the running position rather than to the lower bound and the spacing grows every step.", "All 401, though unevenly, since the grid is laid on a logarithmic scale and the intervals therefore widen with frequency.", "Only those below the first sign change, the scan stopping as soon as it has a bracket to bisect inside.", "28 of the 401, because the scan skips any point at which the end force cannot be evaluated and those are concentrated low down."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The intended spacing is 0.482552133 spm and the first twelve intervals run 0.482552, 0.965104, 1.447656 spm and upward, each a whole multiple of it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 19, 'Which comparison says whether that scan can be trusted on a given string?', '["The intended spacing against the achieved spacing, 0.482552133 spm against 13.028907601 spm, a ratio of 27.000000.", "The engine scan against the dense mode scan, both of which return 53.362124006 spm on the published taper.", "The last point evaluated against the top of the intended range, 3.870312e+4 spm against 195.464155 spm.", "The widest interval inside the range against the smallest gap between two roots, 13.028907601 spm against 90.364754474 spm on the published taper."]'::jsonb, 3, 'Two roots inside one interval leave the sign unchanged at both ends, so what matters is whether any interval is as wide as the gap between modes.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 20, 'ODUMA-4 is refused at 60 spm because its fundamental is 59.134268422 spm. Why can only the joining function raise that refusal?', '["Because the card solver is the only routine that would notice a march failing to settle at a speed that high.", "Because the string module reports a fundamental but has no way to raise an error, its returns being closed form values only.", "It takes the string, the unit, the pump and the fluid together, so it is the only place holding both the speed and the note.", "Because a speed is an input to the pump, where every rate is computed, and the string never sees the number it is being compared with."]'::jsonb, 2, 'A speed is refused because it stands against the string''s own fundamental, which is a reason belonging to two objects at once.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 21, 'A design has a fluid load of 4690.299657 lb and a torque factor of 56.305306799 in. Multiply the two and an in-lb figure appears. Why is it not the gearbox torque?', '["A torque factor is a ds by dtheta in inches per radian, so the product is a torque per radian and has to be divided by the beam sweep of 1.000197032783 rad first.", "A torque factor multiplies the polished rod load, which carries the buoyed rod weight as well, and a fluid load is only the part the plunger hangs on the string.", "The largest torque factor and the largest load do not fall at the same crank angle, so the product is an upper bound no crank angle actually delivers.", "The counterweight has not been subtracted, and a gearbox sees a net torque, so the product is right and simply not yet reduced by the balance."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fluid load is a differential across a plunger area and knows nothing about the 9466.342675 lb of buoyed rod hanging above it.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 22, 'What produces the published unit''s stroke of 106.687716837 in?', '["A beam angle sweep of 1.000197032783 rad multiplied by the front arm of 106.6667 in.", "Twice the crank radius of 28.8 in, scaled by the ratio of the front arm to the rear arm of 64 in.", "The pitman of 80 in swept through the crank angle, the pitman being what carries the crank pin''s motion up to the beam.", "The rear arm of 64 in times the beam sweep, which the front arm then converts into travel at the polished rod."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The stroke is the sweep times the front arm and nothing else, and walking the front arm from 80.0000 to 120.0000 in leaves the ratio at 1.000197032783 throughout.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 23, 'At half a stroke a minute the published taper''s peak polished rod load lands on the buoyed weight plus the fluid load. Which Associate closed forms has that just checked?', '["The spring rate, since a peak that lands on a static sum is what says the compliances were added in series rather than the section stiffnesses.", "The fundamental, since a speed that far below the note is what makes a string behave as a spring, so the check falls on the eigenvalue.", "The surface stroke, since the load at the top of the stroke is the four-bar''s own answer read at the crank angle where the beam stands highest.", "The buoyancy factor and the fluid load together, since a string driven that slowly is only a spring and the rod carries its own buoyed weight plus what the plunger hangs on it."]'::jsonb, 3, 'At 0.5 spm the peak reads 13756.379902 lb against a buoyed weight plus fluid load of 13673.757962 lb, and the minimum reads 8586.448887 lb against a buoyed weight of 8673.757962 lb.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 24, 'Walk the front arm from 80.0000 in to 120.0000 in on the published unit. What moves?', '["The stroke and the upstroke fraction together, from 0.519444444 at the short arm to 0.569444444 at the long one.", "The stroke and the largest torque factor, but not in proportion to each other, since the torque factor is a derivative and the stroke is only a difference.", "Only the size of the motion, since the stroke over the front arm reads 1.000197032783 and the upstroke fraction 0.544444444 at every value.", "Nothing at all, since the front arm sits outside the closure and only the crank, the pitman and the rear arm enter it."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The arm is a pure lever ratio that changes the size of the motion and nothing about its shape or its timing, giving 80.015762623 in of stroke at 80.0000 in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 25, 'The largest torque factor on the published unit is 56.305306799 in. What is it not?', '["A velocity, though it becomes one directly, since the polished rod''s speed is that factor in inches per second at any spm.", "A torque, since it is a length in inches and the closure names no load anywhere to multiply it by.", "A constant of the unit, since it moves from 36.417722862 in to 66.588614078 in as the crank radius is walked.", "A gearbox rating, which is the quantity in in-lb that a designation such as C-320D-200-100 carries at 320000 in-lb."]'::jsonb, 1, 'It becomes a torque only when a load is supplied and a velocity only when a speed is, and the closure supplies neither.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 26, 'What does the designation C-320D-200-100 carry?', '["A gearbox rating of 320000 in-lb, a structural capacity of 20000 lb and a stroke of 100 in, and not one linkage dimension.", "The six lengths of a conventional unit, from which the stroke of 100 in follows once the closure has been taken.", "A front arm, a rear arm and a crank radius scaled to a stroke of 100 in, which is exactly what the generic conventional geometry returns.", "Nothing a design can use, since a designation is parsed only to reject strings that are not designations."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The designation carries ratings a design is checked against, and the package ships no named unit dimensions at all because real beam dimensions are manufacturer data.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 27, 'The linkage samples 360 crank steps and is out by -1.6030e-3 in of stroke; the note scan lays a grid whose widest interval is 13.028907601 spm. What separates the two limits?', '["The linkage''s sampling is the more serious of the two, because a stroke feeds every load while a fundamental is only ever compared against a speed.", "Both cost the same thing, an answer whose error can be bounded but not removed without a finer grid in each case.", "The scan''s grid moves the answer and the linkage''s sampling does not, since a bisection cannot recover a root it has stepped over.", "The linkage''s sampling moves the answer by a known amount, while the scan''s grid leaves the answer exact and costs only the guarantee."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The dense mode scan returns the engine''s own 53.362124006 spm, while the oracle''s Newton closure gives a stroke of 106.689319802 in against the engine''s 106.687716837 in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 28, 'A 1.75 in plunger pulls 962.112750162 lb against 400.0 psi of differential. What does it pull against 800.0 psi?', '["1924.225500324 lb, but the stretch does not double, the string stiffening as it loads.", "More than double, because the plunger has to lift the extra column as well as work against the higher differential.", "1924.225500324 lb, since a fluid load is a differential times an area and is exactly linear in both.", "Less than double, because the buoyancy factor of 0.872611464968 is applied to the load before it reaches the polished rod."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Both columns double to the last figure: the stretch on the published taper goes from 3.602185792 in to 7.204371585 in over the same step.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 29, 'The engine''s PUMP_CONSTANT of 0.116571155977 already contains pi over four. What follows for a caller?', '["It multiplies the plunger area, which is what the in2 in its units is naming, and the diameter squared would apply pi over four twice.", "It multiplies the diameter squared, and feeding it the plunger area applies pi over four a second time.", "It multiplies the swept volume in in3, which it then converts through the 9702 in3 per bbl it is assembled from.", "It multiplies either one, the constant detecting which was supplied."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The area form understates the rated displacement by 21.460184 percent at every plunger size, taking a 1.7500 in plunger from 380.874258458 to 299.137943078 bbl/d.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 30, 'Set the discharge pressure below the intake pressure. What does the pump calculation do?', '["Refuses, with a message about the fluid level, the tubing pressure and the inflow, rather than returning a harmless zero.", "Returns 0.000000 lb of fluid load with a warning code, a plunger with nothing to lift being a design error rather than a parse error.", "Returns the arithmetic answer, a negative fluid load, and leaves the caller to read it as a plunger being held down.", "Returns ok true with the load clamped at 0.000000 lb, so the design can carry on to a rated displacement regardless."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The fluid load that would have been reported reads 0.000000 lb, and that refusal is the only physical check anywhere in the pump calculation.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 31, 'A rated displacement uses a stroke the four-bar computed. Which of the linkage''s answers does the pump take, and which does it leave behind?', '["The surface stroke and the upstroke fraction, since a rating counts strokes over a day and the two halves of a revolution take different lengths of time.", "The surface stroke and the largest torque factor, since the torque factor is what turns a stroke into the velocity the barrel has to fill at.", "The stroke over crank radius ratio of 3.704434612, which is what lets one rating be quoted for any crank the same shape carries.", "The surface stroke and nothing else, leaving the upstroke fraction and every torque factor where they were."]'::jsonb, 3, 'A rating is a constant, a diameter squared, a stroke and a speed, so 0.026449601 bbl per stroke at ten strokes a minute is 380.874258458 bbl/d and the shape of the motion never enters.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 32, 'A design changes the plunger from 1.2500 in to 2.0000 in. Which string quantity follows the pump, and which does not notice?', '["The buoyed weight follows, since a heavier column standing on a wider plunger is carried by the rods and Archimedes is applied to the pair together.", "The fluid load follows, from 2393.010029 lb to 6126.105675 lb, and the stretch it causes with it, while the spring rate and the buoyed weight do not move at all.", "The spring rate follows, since a string''s compliance is worked out under the load it carries and a larger load closes the sections up on themselves.", "Neither moves, since a plunger diameter enters only the rated displacement and the fluid load is fixed by the differential of 1950.0 psi alone."]'::jsonb, 1, 'Fluid load is a differential times a plunger area, and a string''s weight in air, buoyancy factor and compliance sum know nothing whatever about a pump.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 33, 'A 1.7500 in plunger has an area of 2.405281875 in2 and the 7/8 rod above it has 0.601320469 in2. What does that mismatch produce?', '["A taperStepsUp warning, since the section above the plunger is smaller than the plunger the load is developed on.", "A compliance share of 52.427184 percent in the 7/8 section, which is what the ratio of the two areas sets.", "17.560655738 in of stretch on the published taper under a fluid load of 4690.299657039 lb, because the pump end is wide and the string is narrow.", "A buoyancy factor that differs between the plunger and the rods, the fluid acting on two very different areas."]'::jsonb, 2, 'Load and stretch come off the same product: a differential times an area gives 4690.299657039 lb, and that load times the elastic constant gives the stretch.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 34, 'Going from a 1.7500 in plunger to a 2.5000 in plunger on the published unit changes the rating from 380.874258458 to 777.294405015 bbl/d. What is unchanged?', '["The surface stroke of 106.687716837 in, which belongs to six linkage lengths and knows nothing about the pump.", "The fluid load, fixed by the differential of 1950.0 psi and not by the plunger.", "The stretch in the string, since the elastic constant of 3.744037060e-3 in/lb belongs to the steel rather than to the load.", "The volume per stroke, which is set by the stroke alone and is only multiplied out by the plunger when a day is counted."]'::jsonb, 0, 'The load goes from 4690.299657 lb to 9572.040116 lb on the same string, since load and displacement both come off the same square.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 35, 'The order of work says to get the note before choosing a speed. Why in that order?', '["Because the fundamental moves with the speed, and reading it afterwards would give a note for that one speed only.", "Because the note is an input to the compliance sum, which cannot be taken until the eigenvalue has been solved.", "Because the speed sets the time step of the march, and a march at the wrong step raises the timestep code.", "Because a design at or above the string''s fundamental is refused outright, so a speed chosen first may not be allowed at all."]'::jsonb, 3, 'ODUMA-4 asked for 60 spm against 59.134268422 spm returns ok false with one error and no numbers at all.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 36, 'Which check on a worked spring rate costs nothing and catches the commonest error in the subject?', '["That the elastic constant times the spring rate must be one, which catches a compliance inverted at the wrong step.", "That the spring rate must lie between the two section rates of 509.452063888 and 561.436968366 lb/in.", "That the string must be softer than its softest section, which the published taper meets at 267.091373300 lb/in against 509.452063888 lb/in.", "That the spring rate must exceed the softest section''s own rate, which catches sections entered in the wrong order."]'::jsonb, 2, 'An answer larger than the smallest section rate is arithmetic rather than steel, and adding rates on this string gives 1070.889032254 lb/in.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 37, 'Weight in air, buoyed weight, spring rate, fundamental, surface stroke and fluid load. How many of the six move when the speed is doubled?', '["Not one of them, which is what lets this tier be settled before any card has been marched.", "The fundamental alone, a frequency being measured per minute against the same clock the pumping speed is.", "The surface stroke alone, the four-bar being sampled over a revolution that a higher speed passes through faster.", "The fundamental and the fluid load, the differential the plunger works against rising with how hard the well is pumped."]'::jsonb, 0, 'A compliance sum, a buoyancy factor, an eigenvalue, a four-bar closure and a differential times an area carry no time step in them.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 38, 'Three numbers go into the plunger stroke rule on the published taper that needs no march, and each of them belongs to a different object. Which is which?', '["The surface stroke and the elastic constant are both the string''s, a stroke at the pump end being what the string delivers, and only the fluid load comes from elsewhere.", "The surface stroke is the linkage''s, the fluid load is the pump''s and the elastic constant is the string''s, so the rule can be run once those three closed forms are done.", "All three are the string''s, the surface stroke entering as the boundary motion it is driven by and the fluid load as the end condition it is loaded with.", "The surface stroke is the linkage''s, the elastic constant is the pump''s, being the load path from the plunger upward, and the fluid load is the string''s."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The rule is S minus Fo times Er, and 64.000000 in less 18.720185299 in of static stretch gives 45.279814701 in with nothing marched at all.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 39, 'The wave march gives 45.286791250 in at 0.5 spm and 53.042713176 in at 15.0 spm on the published taper. What is the difference between the two answers called?', '["Static stretch, which the march resolves properly and the spring rule can only approximate from the elastic constant.", "Sampling error, since the march reports the peak to trough of a decimated card rather than of every step it took.", "Inertial overtravel, 0.015408 percent above the spring rule at the slow end and 17.144281 percent above it at the fast one.", "The taper factor, which raises the plunger stroke on a stepped string just as it raises the note on one."]'::jsonb, 2, 'The wave answer is longer at every speed on the ladder, and the extra travel is the string still moving when the polished rod turns round.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 40, 'Beside every row of the overtravel ladder sits the speed over the fundamental. What does that column let a reader say that the inches alone do not?', '["Whether the string is being run as a spring or as a wave machine, the overtravel being 0.015408 percent at a ratio of 0.009369942 and 17.144281 percent at 0.281098256.", "Whether the row can be trusted at all, since above about a quarter the march stops settling and the engine raises notPeriodic in place of an answer.", "How close the design sits to the refusal, which is the only use the ratio has, the engine refusing at or above the note and a ratio of one being that refusal as a fraction.", "How much of the surface stroke reaches the plunger, the ratio being what the static stretch in the spring rule is scaled by once the string starts to move."]'::jsonb, 0, 'Overtravel is inertia, so it is a property of how fast a string is driven relative to its own note rather than of the speed in strokes a minute.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 41, 'Two readings of the plunger stroke ladder are on offer: that the spring rule is an approximation the march corrects, and that the two are different quantities. Which is defensible?', '["The first, since the march lands on the rule at half a stroke a minute, and a rule that a finer method reproduces in the limit is by definition an approximation to it, the extra travel being the correction term the static form was missing.", "The first, since the wave answer is longer at every speed on the page, and a rule wrong in one direction everywhere is a rule with a missing correction factor.", "Neither, since the two disagree by 0.006976549 in at 0.5 spm and 7.762898475 in at 15.0 spm, and a disagreement growing that far is a solver artefact.", "The second, with a qualification: the two agree at the static limit, but away from it the march is the travel the barrel sees and the rule has no inertia term to be corrected toward."]'::jsonb, 3, 'The wave answer is what a displacement is fed, and at 0.5 spm the two agree to 0.015408 percent of the spring rule while at 15.0 spm the overtravel is 17.144281 percent.'),
  ('rodpump', 'beginner', 'final', null, 42, 'What does the Associate tier leave for the tiers after it?', '["What the string weighs in fluid, which cannot be settled until the pump has been sized and the fluid load is known.", "What the plunger travels and what the well makes, both of which wait on a solved card.", "What the fundamental is, since a note has no meaning until a speed has been chosen to compare it against.", "What the linkage stroke is, since a stroke cannot be closed until a rod string is hanging from the polished rod."]'::jsonb, 1, 'The fluid load that drives the pump has already stretched the published taper 17.560655738 in before the plunger has moved at all.');
